# uForth Native C/C++ Extension System

Architecture and stable core native functions reference for `natives/`.

> [!NOTE]
> This documentation describes the overall architecture and main core native module (`natives/core.cpp`). For reference on other available native modules (e.g. `display.cpp`, `block.cpp`, `reactor.cpp`, `lora.cpp`, `gps.cpp`), refer directly to the respective `.cpp` files in `natives/`. It is not required to update this document when adding or extending native modules.

---

## 1. Architecture & Dispatch Mechanism

uForth connects Forth code to C/C++ functions via opcode `CALLC` (44):

1. **Registration**: `forth_register("name", fn)` assigns a 1-based ID (up to `MAX_NATIVES` = 64), stores `fn` in `_fns[id]`, and calls `forth_define_native("name", id)` which compiles:
   ```forth
   : name LIT id CALLC EXIT ;
   ```
2. **Dispatch**: When the VM hits `CALLC`, `c_handle()` pops `id` and executes `forth_dispatch(id)` -> `_fns[id]()`.
3. **Two-Phase Registration**:
   - `forth_natives_register_bindings()` inside `natives/natives.cpp` aggregates primitive C++ bindings before `uforth_load_core()`.
   - `forth_natives_register_definitions()` inside `natives/natives.cpp` aggregates high-level Forth word definitions (via `uforth_interpret()`) after `uforth_load_core()`.

---

## 2. Core Native Functions Reference

Stable console I/O, commenting, timing, and dictionary inspection native functions (`natives/core.cpp`).

| Forth Word | C++ Handler | Stack Effect | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `emit` | `fn_emit` | `( char -- )` | Output single ASCII character via `forth_output()` |
| `cr` | `fn_cr` | `( -- )` | Output a newline (`\n`) |
| `.` | `fn_dot` | `( n -- )` | Output 64-bit `DCELL` integer followed by a space |
| `words` | `fn_words` | `( -- )` | Walk dictionary linked list and print all word names |
| `\` | `fn_line_comment` | `( -- )` *(IMM)* | Line comment: skip TIB input to end of line |
| `(` | `fn_paren_comment` | `( -- )` *(IMM)* | Block comment: skip TIB input until closing `)` |
| `key` | `fn_key` | `( -- ascii )` | Blocking key press read (del=8, enter=13, exit=27) |
| `key?` | `fn_key_query` | `( -- ascii \| 0 )` | Non-blocking key press check (returns ASCII code or 0) |
| `ms` | `fn_ms` | `( ms -- )` | Non-blocking RTOS delay in milliseconds (`vTaskDelay`) |
| `us` | `fn_us` | `( us -- )` | Microsecond delay (`delayMicroseconds`) |

### High-Level Core Definitions (`core_definitions`)
| Forth Word | Forth Definition | Stack Effect | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| `count` | `: count dup 1+ swap 0 +c@ ;` | `( cstr -- addr len )` | Convert counted string (`cstr`) to standard Forth `( addr len )` pair |

---

## 3. Creating & Extending Native Modules

To create a new native module in `natives/`:

1. **No Separate `.h` Files**: Do **NOT** create individual per-module `.h` header files (e.g., `lora.h`, `gps.h`, `reactor.h`). Single-purpose header files create redundant `#pragma once` boilerplate and header pollution.
2. **Centralized Internal Contract (`natives_internal.h`)**: Declare module binding and definition prototypes directly inside `natives/natives_internal.h`:
   ```cpp
   void xxx_bindings(void);
   void xxx_definitions(void); // optional
   ```
3. **Single Include**: Each module `.cpp` file in `natives/` includes only:
   ```cpp
   #include "natives_internal.h"
   ```
4. **Implement Handlers**: Implement native C++ primitive handlers reading/writing stack via `dpop()` / `dpush()`.
5. **Two-Phase Registration**:
   - Inside `xxx_bindings()`, register primitive handlers via `forth_register("forth-name", fn)`.
   - Inside `xxx_definitions()`, define high-level Forth words or register reactor services via `uforth_interpret()`.
6. **Aggregate Registration**: Call `xxx_bindings()` inside `forth_natives_register_bindings()` and `xxx_definitions()` inside `forth_natives_register_definitions()` in `natives/natives.cpp`.
7. **String Buffer Standard**: All data buffers written by native modules (e.g. `lora-read`, `gps-pos-str`) must write clean null-terminated C-strings starting directly at `buf[0]` (with `buf[len] = '\0'`). Do not prepend length-prefix bytes.
